<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Betzias HaPas — Introductory Sections (§§ 9–10)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400;">COMPLETE FULL TRANSLATION<br>Continuation of Netilas Yadayim material · Based on <span class="lr">LM II:7</span></span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 9 — The Five Species of Grain, the <i>Hai</i> of <i>HaShishi</i>, and <i>Mezona D'Nishmasa</i></div>

<div class="p">Therefore they are the five species [of grain]. Corresponding to the <span class="h">Hai</span> of <span class="h">HaShishi</span> [the sixth day — in the Torah, the sixth day of creation uniquely has the article <span class="h">Hai</span>: "<span class="h">yom <b>ha</b>shishi</span>"]. For the entire world was suspended until the sixth of Sivan, when Israel received the Torah, which is the aspect of <span class="h">nevu'ah</span> [prophecy], through which the renewal of the world was revealed, as explained in the aforementioned <span class="h">Torah</span> [of Rabbainu].</div>

<div class="p">It emerges that the <span class="h">Hai</span> of <span class="h">HaShishi</span> signifies the renewal of the world. And from there is the essential holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, which receives its holiness through "the power of His deeds," etc. — through knowing that Hashem created the world, the aspect of <span class="v">"In the beginning [He] created,"</span> etc., as explained in the aforementioned <span class="h">Torah</span>. And from there — from the aspect of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, from the aspect of the renewal of the world that was revealed through the <span class="h">Hai</span> of <span class="h">HaShishi</span> — from there we receive the aspect of the five species [of grain], which are the praise of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. For the five species correspond to the <span class="h">Hai</span> of <span class="h">HaShishi</span>, through which the essential holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> [is drawn]. From there the five species receive their power to nourish and satiate a person in the aspect of <span class="h">mezona d'nishmasa</span> [nourishment of the soul], from which the essential true satiation derives, in the aspect of <span class="v">"the <span class="h">tzadik</span> eats to satisfy his soul."</span></div>

<div class="p">Therefore specifically the five species have the power to satiate a person, for they receive their power from <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, from the aspect of the <span class="h">Hai</span> of <span class="h">HaShishi</span>, from the aspect of the faith in the renewal of the world, from which <span class="h">mezona d'nishmasa</span> is drawn — from which the essential satiation [comes]. For in <span class="h">mezona d'gufa</span> [nourishment of the body], which is the aspect of Eisav's eating, eating of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>, it is said: <span class="v">"the belly of the wicked shall be wanting."</span> But <span class="h">mezona d'nishmasa</span> — there is the essential satiation, in the aspect of <span class="v">"the <span class="h">tzadik</span> eats to satisfy his soul."</span> For <span class="h">mezona d'nishmasa</span> is the aspect of <span class="v">"Hashem is my shepherd, I shall not lack,"</span> the aspect of <span class="v">"there is no lack for those who fear Him,"</span> as explained in the aforementioned <span class="h">Torah</span> — the opposite of <span class="h">mezona d'gufa</span>, which is the aspect of the eating of the wicked, which is the aspect of <span class="v">"the belly of the wicked shall be wanting,"</span> as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore also in the physical [realm], the essential satiation is through the five species, which are the primary bread that sustains the heart, upon which one is obligated to say <span class="h">HaMotzi</span> and <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span>. For they are the five species — the praise of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> — into which the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is drawn, the aspect of the faith in the renewal of the world that was revealed through the <span class="h">Hai</span> of <span class="h">HaShishi</span>, on account of which they are five species, as above. And through their being in the aspect of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, etc. — through this they are in the aspect of <span class="h">mezona d'nishmasa</span> even more. Therefore the essential satiation is through them, as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the <span class="h">Hai</span> of <span class="h">HaMotzi</span>. One must enunciate the <span class="h">Hai</span> of "<span class="h">HaMotzi</span>," as our Sages <span class="h">zichronam livrachah</span> cautioned, and the holy Zohar speaks at great length about the matter of the <span class="h">Hai</span> of <span class="h">HaMotzi</span>. For the <span class="h">Hai</span> of "<span class="h"><b>Ha</b>Motzi</span>" is the aspect of the <span class="h">Hai</span> of "<span class="h"><b>Ha</b>Shishi</span>," which signifies the renewal of the world — through which the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> of holy eating in the aspect of <span class="h">mezona d'nishmasa</span> [is achieved], as above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is why our Sages <span class="h">zichronam livrachah</span> linked the blessing of <span class="h">HaMotzi</span> with the <span class="h">Hai</span> to the verse: <span class="v">"<b>Ha</b>Motzi eschem mi'tachas sivlos Mitzrayim"</span> — "Who brings you out from under the burdens of Mitzrayim." For in truth it is all one and the same. For the verse <span class="v">"Who brings you out from under the burdens of Mitzrayim"</span> — i.e. the Exodus from Mitzrayim — signifies the renewal of the world. For at the time of the Exodus, the renewal of the world was revealed through the signs and wonders, as is brought in all the holy books. Moreover, the essential purpose of the Exodus was for the sake of receiving the Torah, as it is written: <span class="v">"when you bring the nation out of Mitzrayim, you shall serve [G-d on this mountain],"</span> etc. And the essential revelation of the renewal of the world was through the receiving of the Torah, as above. It emerges that the verse <span class="v">"Who brings you out from under the burdens of Mitzrayim"</span> signifies the renewal of the world. Therefore from there our Sages <span class="h">zichronam livrachah</span> derived the <span class="h">Hai</span> of <span class="h">HaMotzi</span> — which is the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> — which signifies the renewal of the world, which is the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> of eating in the aspect of <span class="h">mezona d'nishmasa</span>, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 10 — Blessing Over a Whole Loaf</div>

<div class="p">And this is why one must be particular to bless <span class="h">HaMotzi</span> on a whole loaf of bread. And the whole [loaf] has importance and a law of priority over the broken [loaf]. For the eating of a Jew must be in the aspect of <span class="h">mezona d'nishmasa</span>, which is the aspect of completeness [<span class="h">shlaimis</span>] without lack, in the aspect of <span class="v">"Hashem is my shepherd, I shall not lack"</span> — which is said regarding <span class="h">mezona d'nishmasa</span> — the aspect of <span class="v">"there is no lack for those who fear Him."</span> Therefore there is importance to a whole loaf, for it is closer to the aspect of <span class="h">mezona d'nishmasa</span> which is complete, without lack, the aspect of <span class="v">"there is no lack for those who fear Him,"</span> as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore in truth, the apostates of our nation [<span class="h">bnai pritzai amainu</span>] who follow the ways of the idolaters do not leave a whole loaf for the meal, but only slices, in the manner of the idolaters. For in all their mannerisms they wish to demonstrate that they have uprooted themselves from the ways of Israel. For among us, there is importance to a whole loaf for <span class="h">Birchas HaMotzi</span>, as written in the <span class="h">Shulchan Aruch</span>. But those wicked ones reveal that all their nourishment is <span class="h">mezona d'gufa</span> — the aspect of Eisav's eating, which is the eating of the wicked — where there is no completeness. For their eating is in the aspect of <span class="v">"the belly of the wicked shall be wanting"</span> — for they have no <span class="h">emunah</span>. Therefore they do not merit <span class="h">mezona d'nishmasa</span>, which is drawn from <span class="h">emunah</span>, as above.</div>

<div class="p">But the eating of Israel — a holy nation — is in the aspect of <span class="h">mezona d'nishmasa</span>, for they are careful in their blessings to bless Hashem at their eating with <span class="h">Birchas HaMotzi</span> and <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span>. For all of this signifies the renewal of the world. Therefore they are particular to bless over the whole [loaf], which is the aspect of <span class="v">"there is no lack for those who fear Him"</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">mezona d'nishmasa</span>, as above. <span class="kc">Baruch Hashem l'olam, Amain v'Amain.</span></div>

<div class="ts">
  <div class="sh">✦ Translator's Summary — §§ 9–10 ✦</div>
  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;">These two sections form a bridge between the <em>Netilas Yadayim</em> material and the main <em>Betzias HaPas</em> halachah. The core teaching: The five species of grain correspond to the <em>Hai</em> of "<em>HaShishi</em>" — the unique letter in the creation narrative that hints at the Torah's reception on the sixth of Sivan. This <em>Hai</em> = the renewal of the world = the holiness of <em>Eretz Yisrael</em> = <em>mezona d'nishmasa</em> [soul-nourishment]. The five grains satiate because they draw from this root. The <em>Hai</em> of <em>HaMotzi</em> = the <em>Hai</em> of <em>HaShishi</em> = the Exodus = the revelation of <em>chiddush ha'olam</em>. Blessing over a whole loaf = <em>shlaimis</em> = <em>mezona d'nishmasa</em> = "I shall not lack." Those who abandon whole bread abandon <em>emunah</em> itself.</p>
</div>

